CAT = "cisaAudit"; SEG = "AUD"
Q = [
("What is the main purpose of an IS audit charter?",
 "To define audit's authority and scope", ["To list the year's planned audit engagements", "To describe the testing steps for each audit", "To record management's response to findings"],
 "The charter is the mandate: it states the purpose, authority, responsibility and accountability of the audit function. The annual plan, not the charter, lists the engagements."),
("Who should approve the internal IS audit charter?",
 "The board or its audit committee", ["The chief information officer", "The head of the IS audit function", "The external auditor"],
 "Approval by the board or audit committee gives the audit function its authority and protects its independence from the management it audits."),
("What should drive the selection of areas for an annual IS audit plan?",
 "A risk assessment of auditable areas", ["The order used in last year's audit plan", "Requests from IT operations staff", "The areas cheapest to audit"],
 "A risk-based plan puts limited audit hours where the risk to business objectives is greatest, then revisits the ranking as risks change."),
("An IS auditor helped design a payroll system's controls last year and is now asked to audit it. What is the best course of action?",
 "Disclose the conflict and step aside", ["Audit it, since the design is well known", "Audit it, but skip the payroll controls", "Ask the developers to review the work"],
 "Auditing one's own design work impairs independence in fact and in appearance. The auditor should disclose it so the audit can be reassigned."),
("During fieldwork an IS auditor finds signs that a manager may be committing fraud. What should the auditor do first?",
 "Report it to the right level of management", ["Question the manager about the suspect entries", "Expand testing to prove the fraud", "Notify the police straight away"],
 "Standards call for prompt communication of suspected fraud to an appropriate level of management or those charged with governance, usually above the person suspected. Investigating alone can tip off the suspect and damage evidence."),
("An IS auditor needs evidence that password length rules are enforced on a server. Which source is most reliable?",
 "Settings the auditor views on the server", ["A screenshot sent by the administrator", "The administrator's verbal account", "The password policy document itself"],
 "Evidence the auditor obtains directly is more reliable than evidence relayed by the auditee, and a policy shows only what should happen, not what is configured."),
("A month-end reconciliation that flags differences between two ledgers after posting is which type of control?",
 "Detective", ["Preventive", "Deterrent", "Directive"],
 "The reconciliation finds errors after they occur, so it is detective. A preventive control, such as an input edit, would stop the error from being posted."),
("In a small IT department one person both develops and deploys code. Which compensating control is most effective?",
 "Supervisor review of deployment logs", ["Annual secure coding training for the developer", "A stronger password for that person", "A signed acceptable use policy"],
 "When duties cannot be separated, a detective control performed by someone else, such as a supervisor reviewing what was moved to production, reduces the risk of unauthorized changes."),
("An IS auditor expects almost no exceptions and wants to find at least one if any exist, as in a fraud review. Which sampling method fits best?",
 "Discovery sampling", ["Stratified variable sampling", "Haphazard sampling", "Monetary unit sampling"],
 "Discovery sampling is a form of attribute sampling used when the expected deviation rate is very low; it sizes the sample to give a set chance of finding one occurrence."),
("Which sampling approach lets an auditor end testing early when the first results show very few deviations?",
 "Stop-or-go sampling", ["Judgmental sampling", "Cluster sampling", "Systematic sampling"],
 "Stop-or-go (sequential) sampling tests in stages and allows the auditor to stop once results support a conclusion, avoiding oversampling when few errors appear."),
("What is the main advantage of statistical sampling over judgmental sampling?",
 "Sampling risk can be quantified", ["It always needs a smaller sample", "It removes the need for auditor judgment", "It examines every item in the population"],
 "Statistical sampling lets the auditor quantify the probability that the sample misrepresents the population. Judgment is still needed to set confidence and tolerable error."),
("Before running an audit analytics script on a data extract supplied by IT, what should the IS auditor do first?",
 "Confirm the extract is complete", ["Run the script on a small test file", "Ask IT to run the script for audit", "Agree the report format with the auditee"],
 "Conclusions are only as good as the data. Reconciling record counts and control totals to the source system confirms the extract is complete before any analysis."),
("Which audit technique processes fictitious transactions for a dummy entity alongside live data in the production system?",
 "An integrated test facility", ["Parallel simulation", "A snapshot technique", "Generalized audit software (GAS)"],
 "An integrated test facility sets up a fictitious entity in live files and runs test transactions against it, so results can be compared with expected values. The dummy entries must be reversed so they do not affect real reports."),
("Which continuous audit technique embeds routines in an application to capture transactions that meet audit-set criteria?",
 "An embedded audit module", ["An integrated test facility", "A parallel simulation", "A base case system evaluation"],
 "An embedded audit module, sometimes called a systems control audit review file, sits in the application and writes selected transactions to an audit file for later review."),
("Continuous auditing techniques are most useful in which environment?",
 "High-volume, paperless systems", ["Systems with few, high-value transactions", "Offline systems run once a quarter", "Manual processes with full paper records"],
 "Where transactions are many and evidence exists only electronically, continuous techniques let the auditor test as processing happens rather than after the trail is gone."),
("An IS auditor writes a program that recalculates commission on real sales data and compares the result with the production output. What is this technique called?",
 "Parallel simulation", ["Test data", "Tagging and tracing", "Code comparison"],
 "In parallel simulation the auditor's own program processes actual data and the outputs are compared. The test data method does the reverse: auditor data through the production program."),
("Management disagrees with an audit finding during the closing meeting. What should the IS auditor do?",
 "Report it with management's response", ["Remove the finding from the report", "Delay the report until both sides agree", "Soften the finding to reach agreement"],
 "The auditor stays objective: a finding supported by evidence is reported, and management's view is included so readers see both. Evidence, not agreement, decides what is reported."),
("What is the main purpose of an exit meeting at the end of audit fieldwork?",
 "To confirm the facts behind findings", ["To agree the next year's audit scope and plan", "To collect remaining audit evidence", "To approve the final audit budget"],
 "Discussing findings before the report is issued lets the auditee correct factual errors and start planning corrective action, which makes the final report accurate."),
("Who is responsible for implementing the corrective actions agreed in response to an IS audit report?",
 "Auditee management", ["The IS audit team", "The external auditor", "The audit committee"],
 "Management owns its controls and the fixes. The auditor's role is to follow up and report on whether agreed actions were taken, which keeps the auditor independent."),
("Management decides not to fix a high-risk audit finding and to accept the risk. What should the IS auditor do?",
 "Have senior management sign off on it", ["Fix the control on management's behalf", "Close the finding without further action", "Repeat the audit until it is fixed"],
 "Management may accept risk, but the decision should be documented by someone with authority to do so. If the risk exceeds appetite, the auditor escalates it to the audit committee."),
("The auditee refuses to give an IS auditor access to a critical server's configuration files. What should the auditor do?",
 "Report the scope limitation", ["Leave the server out without comment", "Rely on the administrator's word", "Obtain the files without permission"],
 "A scope limitation can affect the audit opinion, so it is raised with management and the audit committee and disclosed in the report."),
("What is the main benefit of a control self-assessment program?",
 "Owners take charge of their controls", ["It replaces the internal audit function", "It removes the need for external audit", "It shifts control duties to auditors"],
 "Control self-assessment has process owners evaluate their own controls, raising awareness and catching problems early. It supports audit work but does not replace it."),
("Which evidence is weakest when relied on alone?",
 "A staff member's verbal description", ["A system-generated audit log", "An auditor's reperformance result", "A signed confirmation from a third party"],
 "Inquiry alone is weak evidence because it may not reflect what happens. It should be corroborated by observation, inspection or reperformance."),
("What is the main limitation of observing a control being performed?",
 "It only shows the control at that moment", ["It cannot be documented in workpapers", "It always needs the auditor to reperform", "It produces no evidence the auditor can use"],
 "People may act differently while watched, and observation covers only the point in time seen. Other tests are needed for the rest of the period."),
("What is the main purpose of a walk-through at the start of an IS audit?",
 "To confirm how the process works", ["To test controls over the full period", "To decide the audit opinion", "To sample transactions for errors"],
 "Tracing one transaction from start to end confirms how the process and its controls actually work, which shapes the rest of the audit plan."),
("Checking that a sample of user access requests carries an approver's signature is what kind of test?",
 "A compliance test", ["A substantive test", "A load test", "A recovery test"],
 "A compliance test checks whether a control is applied as designed. A substantive test checks the data itself, such as whether balances are correct."),
("Compliance testing shows that input controls on a billing system often fail. How should the IS auditor respond?",
 "Increase substantive testing", ["Reduce substantive testing", "Rely on the controls anyway", "Stop the audit at that point"],
 "When controls cannot be relied on, the risk of errors in the data rises, so the auditor gathers more direct evidence about the data itself."),
("What is the most important quality of IS audit workpapers?",
 "They support the audit conclusions", ["They are stored only on paper", "They are shared in full with the auditee", "They follow last year's layout"],
 "Workpapers should hold enough evidence that another qualified auditor could reach the same conclusion. Format and storage matter less than sufficiency."),
("An IS auditor plans to use a penetration test report from an outside firm. What should the auditor do first?",
 "Assess the firm's skills and independence", ["Copy its findings straight into the report", "Rerun the whole penetration test", "Ask the firm to sign the audit report"],
 "Before relying on another party's work, the auditor evaluates their competence, objectivity and scope, and decides how much of the work can be used."),
("What is the main purpose of a quality assurance review of completed IS audit engagements?",
 "To confirm the work met standards", ["To test the auditee's controls again", "To approve the auditee's action plans", "To measure auditor time sheets only"],
 "Quality assurance checks that engagements followed professional standards and the function's methodology, and feeds improvements back into the audit process."),
("When does an IS control weakness become material?",
 "When it could defeat a control objective", ["When it costs more than a set amount", "When the auditee agrees that it is serious", "When it was found in a prior audit"],
 "Materiality in IS audit is judged by impact on control objectives, often from the combined effect of weaknesses, not only by a monetary figure."),
("An IS auditor obtains customers' personal data during an audit. What does the ISACA Code of Professional Ethics require?",
 "Use it only for the audit's purpose", ["Delete it as soon as fieldwork ends", "Share it with any manager who asks", "Publish a summary in the report"],
 "The Code requires auditors to keep information confidential unless disclosure is legally required, and not to use it for personal benefit."),
("Analytics shows 40 vendor records sharing bank details with employee payroll records. What should the IS auditor do next?",
 "Validate the matches before reporting", ["Report all 40 as confirmed fraud cases", "Delete the vendor records found", "Ask payroll to stop all payments"],
 "Analytics produces exceptions, not conclusions. Some matches may be legitimate, so the auditor validates them before reporting."),
("A control weakness found during fieldwork is fixed by management before the report is issued. How should the IS auditor treat it?",
 "Report it and note it was fixed", ["Leave it out of the report", "Delay the report until it is retested", "Report it as still open"],
 "The weakness existed during the period audited, so it is reported. Noting that it was corrected gives readers the full picture."),
("Audit time is cut by a third midway through the plan. What should the IS auditor do?",
 "Cover the highest-risk areas first", ["Drop the audits at the end of the list", "Shorten every audit by a third", "Skip areas audited last year"],
 "A risk-based approach means the remaining hours go to the areas of greatest risk, with the reduced coverage reported to the audit committee."),
("What should an IS audit report link each finding to?",
 "Its risk to the business", ["The auditor who found it", "The cost of the audit", "The audit software used"],
 "Readers act on findings that explain the impact on business objectives, along with the cause, the criteria and a recommendation."),
]
